'''The 2008–09 season''' was the 115th season of competitive [[football (soccer)|football]] by [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]].

==Overview==
Celtic went into the 2008–09 season defending their [[Scottish Premier League]] title, which they won for the third consecutive time in [[2007–08 Scottish Premier League|2007–08]].

They also entered the [[UEFA Champions League]] directly at the group stage, as well as taking part in the two domestic cup competitions, the [[Scottish Cup]] and the [[Scottish League Cup]].

To prepare for the Season, Celtic took part in two Pre-Season Tournaments; the Algarve Challenge Cup in [[Faro, Portugal|Faro]], Portugal, where they finished bottom, having faced [[Middlesbrough F.C.|Middlesbrough]] and [[Cardiff City F.C.|Cardiff City]], and the [[Feyenoord Jubilee Tournament]] in [[Rotterdam]], where they finished second, having faced [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] and host club [[Feyenoord]].

==The campaign==

===League campaign===
The Championship flag was unfurled before Celtic's first League game, against [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]] at [[Celtic Park]] on 10 August by Rosemary Burns, the [[widow]] of former Celtic player [[Tommy Burns (footballer)|Tommy Burns]], who had died of [[skin cancer]] at the end of [[2007–08 Celtic F.C. season|season 2007–08]]. Celtic won the game 1–0, through a [[Barry Robson]] [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]], getting their campaign off to a winning start.

Celtic finished second in the [[Scottish Premier League|SPL]] table at the end of the season, eventually finishing four points behind champions [[Rangers FC|Rangers]]. This was despite at one point having built up a seven-point lead over Rangers. This lead was squandered though, Celtic dropping points to [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]], [[Inverness Caledonian Thistle F.C.|Inverness Caledonian Thistle]], [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]], Rangers themselves, and on two occasions to both [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] and [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]].

One day after the final league game of the season, a 0–0 draw with Hearts at [[Celtic Park]], it was announced that [[Gordon Strachan]] would leave as manager of Celtic with immediate effect. This brought to an end Strachan's four-year spell as manager, during which he guided the club to three SPL titles, one [[Scottish Cup]] and two [[Scottish League Cup]]s. He also guided the club twice into the last 16 of the [[UEFA Champions League|Champions League]], losing out to [[AC Milan]] and [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] respectively.

===European campaign===
Celtic, as Scottish champions, qualified directly for the [[2008–09 UEFA Champions League|UEFA Champions League]], where they were the sole Scottish side as [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] were knocked out in the second qualifying round by [[FBK Kaunas]], meaning Celtic received all the TV money allocated to Scotland.

Celtic were in Pot 3 for the group stage draw, which took place in [[Monaco]] on 28 August. They were drawn with [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]], [[Villarreal CF|Villarreal]] and [[AaB Football|Aalborg]]. The first game, a home tie against Aalborg, finished in a goalless draw. The next game, away to Villarreal, ended in a 1–0 defeat, which was followed by a 3–0 defeat away to Manchester United. Next up, at home, Celtic drew 1–1 with Manchester United, with [[Scott McDonald]] putting Celtic in the lead in the 13th minute, only for [[Ryan Giggs]] to equalise for Manchester United in the 84th minute. A disappointing 2–1 defeat to Aalborg in Denmark saw Celtic's European aspirations end, despite having taken the lead through [[Barry Robson]]. Nonetheless, Celtic rallied to end their campaign on a high with a 2–0 victory over Villarreal at Celtic Park.

===Domestic cups===
Celtic entered the [[Scottish League Cup]] at the third round proper (the last 16 stage) and the [[Scottish Cup]] at the fourth round proper (the Round of 32). The club beat [[Livingston F.C.|Livingston]] 4–0 in the third round of the League Cup on 23 September, and travelled to [[Kilmarnock F.C.|Kilmarnock]] on 28 October, beating them 3–1 and qualifying for the semi-finals, where they faced [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]] at [[Hampden Park]] on 28 January. After a 0–0 draw in extra time, Celtic won a penalty shoot-out 11–10 to set up a final against [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] on 15 March. The team won the [[2009 Scottish League Cup Final]] 2–0 [[after extra time]] at Hampden, with [[Darren O'Dea]] and [[Aiden McGeady]] getting the goals.

The fourth round of the Scottish Cup took place on 10 January, with Celtic beating [[Dundee F.C.|Dundee]] at Celtic Park. In the fifth round, Celtic hosted [[Queen's Park F.C.|Queen's Park]] on 7 February, beating them 2–1. In the quarter-finals, Celtic lost 1–0 at [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]] on 7 March.

